1
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:21,405
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, there's always this gap between self and others, other things.

2
00:00:21,425 --> 00:00:23,726
[SPEAKER_00]: There's never... it can never be filled.

3
00:00:25,127 --> 00:00:27,048
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, you can never get to anything.

4
00:00:27,789 --> 00:00:28,949
[SPEAKER_00]: That self can never own anything.

5
00:00:28,969 --> 00:00:35,613
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always this abyss that can never be crossed.

6
00:00:37,435 --> 00:00:59,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that gap is the result of you assuming the sense of self, which obviously automatically means assuming the self of others, or rather

7
00:01:00,952 --> 00:01:03,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Assuming self internally, assuming self externally.

8
00:01:04,215 --> 00:01:12,983
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as that assumption is given the priority, as long as it's the center of your acts and thinking, there will be a gap generated as a result of it.

9
00:01:14,783 --> 00:01:18,225
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how things are.

10
00:01:18,345 --> 00:01:21,366
[SPEAKER_01]: It is a mistake, so to speak, but it's a beginningless mistake.

11
00:01:21,926 --> 00:01:26,888
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the gap is real, and the sense of self is real, and the sense of others is also real.

12
00:01:27,629 --> 00:01:28,829
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just have to reverse it.

13
00:01:29,209 --> 00:01:37,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of me trying to fill this gap, trying to possess this gap, trying to acquire things and others, or whatever else, you realize

14
00:01:39,836 --> 00:01:41,438
[SPEAKER_01]: that the whole picture is upside down.

15
00:01:41,558 --> 00:01:44,262
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's actually you are the result.

16
00:01:45,323 --> 00:01:51,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Your sense of self, sense of others, is the result of the gap that's impossible to own.

17
00:01:52,833 --> 00:01:54,775
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not me reaching out to

18
00:01:57,315 --> 00:01:59,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Possess the gap, to fill the gap.

19
00:01:59,656 --> 00:02:10,101
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically gap defining me as... as... as... well, as that being that's in lack, that's lacking, that's in the need, and so on.

20
00:02:11,362 --> 00:02:24,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you recognize that actually you are secondary, your sense of self is secondary to the gap, and you start seeing gap as a phenomenon, then you... well, you start reaching for it less and less, because you recognize actually reaching is the result of the gap.

21
00:02:25,073 --> 00:02:29,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that reaching is the beginning, where you kind of try to get the gap and fill it.

22
00:02:31,338 --> 00:02:34,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's a... You feel the need to fill because the gap is there.

23
00:02:35,841 --> 00:02:39,164
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you appropriate the gap, you appropriate the need to fill.

24
00:02:39,564 --> 00:02:46,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Or if you appropriate the possibility of that gap being filled through your assumption, you will then crave to fill the gap.

25
00:02:46,990 --> 00:02:52,655
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that would be possible if the gap hasn't arisen there beforehand, on its own, manifested, arisen to you.

26
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:57,322
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you recognize that this need to fill it, it's because it has arisen as such.

27
00:02:58,882 --> 00:02:59,642
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not yours.

28
00:02:59,702 --> 00:03:02,284
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not you filling it, or trying... you trying to fill.

29
00:03:02,304 --> 00:03:03,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Or that it needs to be.

30
00:03:04,164 --> 00:03:04,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

31
00:03:04,444 --> 00:03:05,845
[SPEAKER_01]: The gap arises as a gap.

32
00:03:06,325 --> 00:03:06,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

33
00:03:06,525 --> 00:03:10,107
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then the need to fill doesn't belong to anybody, and then you stop filling it.

34
00:03:13,028 --> 00:03:13,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

35
00:03:13,748 --> 00:03:18,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is need for oneness, or the idea that oneness... That's only the result of the separation.

36
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:18,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

37
00:03:20,451 --> 00:03:22,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Like unity implies separation.

38
00:03:23,687 --> 00:03:34,755
[SPEAKER_01]: As much as separation implies the possibility of unity, but abandoning both means none of it is applicable anymore.

39
00:03:34,935 --> 00:03:44,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people would think they have unity, but it's only because they maintain the idea of separation through craving for unity.

40
00:03:45,462 --> 00:03:48,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you have to abandon both.

41
00:03:49,399 --> 00:03:59,423
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to see that whether it's separating or united, or both separate or united, neither separate nor united, it's irrelevant and it's determined by the presence of the gap beforehand.

42
00:04:01,984 --> 00:04:03,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of the senses beforehand.

43
00:04:05,505 --> 00:04:06,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't stop that.

44
00:04:06,925 --> 00:04:08,026
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't stop that.

45
00:04:08,546 --> 00:04:12,747
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say beforehand, I mean structurally before, not temporarily before.

46
00:04:14,853 --> 00:04:16,173
[SPEAKER_01]: So, things arise on their own.

47
00:04:16,794 --> 00:04:18,574
[SPEAKER_01]: They manifest, including your thoughts.

48
00:04:18,594 --> 00:04:19,895
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, obviously, you appropriate them.

49
00:04:20,235 --> 00:04:26,717
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume that the ownership, in hindsight, in retrospect, that was mine.

50
00:04:26,817 --> 00:04:30,759
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that things arise on their own means you're not their creator.

51
00:04:30,919 --> 00:04:31,879
[SPEAKER_01]: They arise to you.

52
00:04:32,319 --> 00:04:35,560
[SPEAKER_01]: You might feel that they're arising for you, and so on.

53
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:37,661
[SPEAKER_01]: But all of that is secondary to their arising.

54
00:04:42,913 --> 00:05:00,759
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's seen like that, then the whole idea of unity, separation, filling the gap, craving for it, impossible, insatiable, you can't satisfy desires or whatever else, all that is the result of the things that have already arisen and you appropriate them.

55
00:05:00,839 --> 00:05:07,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you appropriate them, means you fall under... well, you become a victim, so to speak, of their inherent pressure.

56
00:05:08,995 --> 00:05:10,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the pressure of the senses.

57
00:05:10,516 --> 00:05:12,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Senses are like wild animals.

58
00:05:12,197 --> 00:05:14,038
[SPEAKER_01]: They want to go and feed where they want to go and feed.

59
00:05:14,919 --> 00:05:17,501
[SPEAKER_01]: You take them to be yours, means you are going there to feed.

60
00:05:18,381 --> 00:05:19,902
[SPEAKER_01]: They are dragging you left and right.

61
00:05:20,843 --> 00:05:28,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a crocodile, a monkey, wild boar, all those similes are for the senses.

62
00:05:29,909 --> 00:05:30,129
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

63
00:05:30,610 --> 00:05:31,771
[SPEAKER_01]: But they do that on their own.

64
00:05:31,831 --> 00:05:32,671
[SPEAKER_01]: That's their business.

65
00:05:33,232 --> 00:05:34,673
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just you are interfering with it.

66
00:05:36,914 --> 00:05:37,955
[SPEAKER_01]: You are trying to own it.

67
00:05:38,722 --> 00:05:41,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, because they're stronger than you, they'll take you with you.

68
00:05:41,946 --> 00:05:42,587
[SPEAKER_01]: With them, sorry.

69
00:05:43,508 --> 00:05:46,811
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're stronger than them, then you'll be in control of the senses.

70
00:05:46,871 --> 00:05:51,477
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only way to be stronger than your senses is to let go of them, to recognize for what they are.

71
00:05:51,957 --> 00:05:55,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, that is possible to do only once you have restrained them.

72
00:05:56,022 --> 00:05:57,684
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sīla comes first.

73
00:06:02,462 --> 00:06:29,587
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the whole idea of filling that gap, which just becomes inconceivable, because that gap was only the result of maintaining that mistake of ownership, that then, in return, would be feeding more of the desire for ownership, because it's obvious that you can't own, but you took the whole... like... you took it... the entire situation is taken in an upside-down manner, and that's... and that is taken for granted.

74
00:06:30,427 --> 00:06:41,193
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how much you kind of... your experience shows you that you can't fill that gap, that you can't own things, you never really question that assumption.

75
00:06:41,293 --> 00:06:42,174
[SPEAKER_01]: You just keep doing it.

76
00:06:42,614 --> 00:06:44,555
[SPEAKER_01]: You just keep each time and you can't help it.

77
00:06:44,615 --> 00:06:51,640
[SPEAKER_01]: And even you say, yeah, my desires cannot be satisfied, but you can't help going after them, because the five senses are pulling you and you're too weak.

78
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:57,303
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to start realizing, hold on, what if I've taken this completely the wrong way around?

79
00:06:58,436 --> 00:07:00,437
[SPEAKER_01]: What if it's not supposed to be filled?

80
00:07:00,517 --> 00:07:07,719
[SPEAKER_01]: And what if that whole idea of filling this gap and these desires is the result of me trying to fill the gap and the desires?

81
00:07:08,439 --> 00:07:08,579
Yeah.

82
00:07:09,039 --> 00:07:11,420
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you constantly trying will make you want to try.

83
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:12,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a vicious circle.

84
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:13,880
[SPEAKER_01]: It just goes around.

85
00:07:14,260 --> 00:07:16,521
[SPEAKER_01]: But the entire thing is entirely gratuitous.

86
00:07:16,541 --> 00:07:17,421
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just there's nothing.

87
00:07:17,881 --> 00:07:19,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there could be a desire

88
00:07:21,447 --> 00:07:32,749
[SPEAKER_00]: If you look at marriage and relationships like that, the desire to be united with the other person, the love, this constant craving... Yeah, there's no problem with craving.

89
00:07:33,409 --> 00:07:46,311
[SPEAKER_01]: The purest of the world, well, any form of love, for that matter, actually, it's just absolutely rooted in attachment, rooted in craving, rooted in that assumption of ownership.

90
00:07:47,191 --> 00:07:50,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, there is pure love, and pure love is pretty much craving.

91
00:07:53,257 --> 00:08:00,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Because once that's in play, that love, that desire to... for oneness, there's suffering constantly.

92
00:08:00,703 --> 00:08:00,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

93
00:08:01,323 --> 00:08:06,007
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as you take that assumption for granted, means you will keep trying.

94
00:08:06,968 --> 00:08:13,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you never... you will never take all of the signs of your experience that are showing you that you can't actually fill that gap, nor can you satisfy your desires.

95
00:08:14,534 --> 00:08:21,299
[SPEAKER_01]: You will never take that as a... as a sign, as an indication that actually, hold on, maybe it's impossible.

96
00:08:22,308 --> 00:08:29,072
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you do, you don't take it on a deeper level, whereby it will make you stop going after it.

97
00:08:29,092 --> 00:08:40,039
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, people can, on a shallow level, on a shallow mental level, they can realize, oh yes, sure, yeah, of course, I mean, the life is pointless and you can't really satisfy your desires.

98
00:08:40,059 --> 00:08:41,100
[SPEAKER_01]: But that won't sink in.

99
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:41,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

100
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:43,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they don't make enough effort for it to sink in.

101
00:08:44,622 --> 00:08:45,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And why?

102
00:08:45,222 --> 00:08:46,523
[SPEAKER_01]: More importantly, because they don't

103
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:53,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Develop their behavior beforehand, as in practice virtue, restraint and sīla, then it will sink in.

104
00:08:53,606 --> 00:08:56,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it can't sink in, because their behavior contradicts it.

105
00:08:57,690 --> 00:08:58,891
[SPEAKER_01]: The behavior goes against it.

106
00:08:58,931 --> 00:09:12,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how much mentally you might be reflecting about how the unsatisfying pursuit of desire is there, if your acts are still aimed at the pursuit of desire, it means, like, whatever you're thinking won't bear much significance, because there's nowhere to land.

107
00:09:13,146 --> 00:09:19,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Your actions are just swiping it all away, just poof, immediately, because they're still going full power after desires.

108
00:09:20,013 --> 00:09:30,605
[SPEAKER_01]: So you actually have to stop that first, endure that initial pain, and then these reflections will be able to sink in and land on the proper ground that you created through restraint.

109
00:09:44,736 --> 00:09:46,156
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the important thing to remember.

110
00:09:47,537 --> 00:10:01,922
[SPEAKER_01]: The craving, the desire to fill the gap, is basically the result of the desire to fill the gap.

111
00:10:15,668 --> 00:10:26,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Because something can lack something else, only in regard to a different thing, if you know what I mean.

112
00:10:28,152 --> 00:10:34,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a gap is to be filled only in regard to a filled gap.

113
00:10:35,475 --> 00:10:40,857
[SPEAKER_01]: In itself, an empty hole, a gap, it's a thing in itself.

114
00:10:42,177 --> 00:10:43,518
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's no desire to be filled.

115
00:10:45,472 --> 00:10:52,636
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you project the assumption of a full hole, filled hole, leveled hole, completed hole, then this will seem incomplete.

116
00:10:53,717 --> 00:10:58,440
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the... and because you appropriate it, then you feel the pressure of that incompleteness.

117
00:10:58,520 --> 00:11:00,861
[SPEAKER_01]: You too... because you... you stick your hand in it.

118
00:11:01,922 --> 00:11:02,582
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't yours.

119
00:11:02,622 --> 00:11:06,024
[SPEAKER_01]: You just wanted... you just don't know how to not make it yours.

120
00:11:06,445 --> 00:11:09,106
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you just become a victim of that pressure of fill it.

121
00:11:09,747 --> 00:11:10,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Fill that gap.

122
00:11:10,447 --> 00:11:11,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Satisfy those desires.

123
00:11:13,279 --> 00:11:21,924
[SPEAKER_01]: But in itself, as I said, like we spoke before, an itch, physical itch that you want to scratch, in itself, it's just an itch.

124
00:11:23,565 --> 00:11:31,650
[SPEAKER_01]: In comparison to the state that it's free of itch, oh, you feel pressure of scratching it, because scratching it might make it stop.

125
00:11:32,110 --> 00:11:35,312
[SPEAKER_01]: The itch might bring it to an end, which is more pleasant.

126
00:11:36,067 --> 00:11:38,328
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you follow more pleasure, basically.

127
00:11:38,488 --> 00:11:50,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so, having a non-desire, having a satisfied desire, it's more pleasant than having a desire that is not satisfied, that is more unpleasant.

128
00:11:51,492 --> 00:12:04,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will always pursue the satisfaction of desire, no matter how futile it is, no matter how much you know that it's futile, you will pursue it for as long as you hold the assumption of the pleasure that you get through desires.

129
00:12:05,375 --> 00:12:09,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you always go after it, because it's based on emotional level.

130
00:12:10,297 --> 00:12:11,137
[SPEAKER_01]: You pursue pleasure.

131
00:12:12,217 --> 00:12:18,379
[SPEAKER_01]: So, filling a gap, you keep doing it, because you assume that doing it is more pleasant than not doing it.

132
00:12:19,220 --> 00:12:22,721
[SPEAKER_01]: But you fail to see that through doing it, you maintain that discrepancy.

133
00:12:23,661 --> 00:12:28,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you keep projecting the discrepancy that's created by you wanting

134
00:12:29,338 --> 00:12:34,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasurable experience of a filled gap, as opposed to a gap that's not filled.

135
00:12:34,519 --> 00:12:37,300
[SPEAKER_01]: A hole, that it's hollow, that it's empty.

136
00:12:38,541 --> 00:12:43,282
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you stop holding on to the assumption of a filled gap, then the discrepancy won't be hurting you anymore.

137
00:12:43,662 --> 00:12:45,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there is no more pressure of desire.

138
00:12:46,003 --> 00:12:48,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Because an empty hole, it's a thing in itself.

139
00:12:48,623 --> 00:12:50,024
[SPEAKER_01]: A full hole, it's a thing in itself.

140
00:12:51,804 --> 00:12:52,444
[SPEAKER_00]: A perception.

141
00:12:52,985 --> 00:12:53,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

142
00:12:54,345 --> 00:12:58,186
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, that's why, that's why, like in the in the simile of scratching the edge

143
00:12:59,112 --> 00:13:05,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you maintain the itch through constantly scratching it, because through scratching it, you prevent it from healing.

144
00:13:05,894 --> 00:13:09,175
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep maintaining that discrepancy through your acts of scratching.

145
00:13:09,476 --> 00:13:10,556
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, it will keep itching.

146
00:13:11,376 --> 00:13:12,257
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I meant before.

147
00:13:12,457 --> 00:13:14,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Through pursuit of desire, you maintain desire.

148
00:13:15,518 --> 00:13:16,358
[SPEAKER_01]: It's as simple as that.

149
00:13:17,938 --> 00:13:24,341
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of, like, perceptions, in pursuit of perceptions... Yeah, you perceive those, those... Getting involved.

150
00:13:24,681 --> 00:13:27,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Those... that perception of a filled hole,

151
00:13:28,970 --> 00:13:35,034
[SPEAKER_01]: of a complete desire, it's inciting, it's pleasant, as opposed to the incomplete.

152
00:13:36,215 --> 00:13:39,257
[SPEAKER_01]: And because you crave for pleasure, you go after that perception.

153
00:13:41,379 --> 00:13:47,523
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop craving for pleasure, then, yeah, there is a more agreeable perception, there is a more disagreeable perception, but none of them

154
00:13:49,453 --> 00:13:51,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Need to be appropriated because of that.

155
00:13:51,595 --> 00:14:01,626
[SPEAKER_01]: And none of them will be appropriated if you sort of relinquish the idea of that sensual pleasure that you get through pursuit of such perceptions.

156
00:14:01,646 --> 00:14:06,411
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was said it's impossible to engage in sensuality without sensual perceptions.

157
00:14:08,354 --> 00:14:24,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Because some people were claiming, saying, oh, you can, you know, you can be free from sensuality and still engage, I don't know, in an intercourse or various things, because there were loads of Brahmins and ascetics sort of claiming the ultimate nibbāna, yet, you know, having wives and whatever else.

158
00:14:25,501 --> 00:14:33,744
[SPEAKER_01]: They would say it's impossible, it's inconceivable to be able to engage in sensuality, in an act of sensuality, without having those sensual perceptions established before it.

159
00:14:34,044 --> 00:14:36,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And as I said, the sensual perceptions are the distortion

160
00:14:40,015 --> 00:14:41,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you assume, as such, as real?

161
00:14:42,155 --> 00:14:43,376
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's all you have at the top.

162
00:14:49,417 --> 00:14:59,839
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a... there's a... an edga sappaya sutta, 106, majjhima, in regard to the base of nothingness.

163
00:15:02,179 --> 00:15:04,700
[SPEAKER_00]: There's three ways to attain the base of nothingness.

164
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:05,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

165
00:15:05,820 --> 00:15:06,860
[SPEAKER_00]: And it says... it goes on...

166
00:15:08,178 --> 00:15:14,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, Bhikkhu is a noble disciple, considers thus essential pleasures here and now, and essential pleasures in life in life to come.

167
00:15:15,659 --> 00:15:18,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Essential pleasures here and now, and essential pleasures in life to come.

168
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:23,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, essential perceptions here and now, essential perceptions in life to come.

169
00:15:24,521 --> 00:15:27,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Material forms here and now, material forms in life to come.

170
00:15:28,242 --> 00:15:31,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception of forms here and now, perceptions of forms in life to come.

171
00:15:32,382 --> 00:15:35,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Perceptions of the imperturbable and air-generated.

172
00:15:37,128 --> 00:15:39,850
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands it.

173
00:15:39,890 --> 00:15:40,911
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands it.

174
00:16:03,782 --> 00:16:05,623
[SPEAKER_00]: The rest is a kind of strange translation.

175
00:16:05,743 --> 00:16:14,068
[SPEAKER_00]: On the dissolution of the body after death, it is possible that the evolving consciousness... That's another dodgy translation.

176
00:16:14,088 --> 00:16:17,389
[SPEAKER_00]: ...may pass on to rebirth in the base of nothingness.

177
00:16:18,390 --> 00:16:22,012
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the first way directed to the base of nothingness.

178
00:16:23,273 --> 00:16:26,254
[SPEAKER_00]: The first way that you can attain this base of nothingness.

179
00:16:26,895 --> 00:16:32,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So, where is this... where these perceptions cease without remainder, that is peaceful.

180
00:16:33,718 --> 00:16:34,818
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the base of nothingness.

181
00:16:35,938 --> 00:16:40,059
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you... where is that base of nothingness?

182
00:16:41,019 --> 00:16:42,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the perceptions are absent.

183
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:44,740
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the thing.

184
00:16:45,500 --> 00:17:00,583
[SPEAKER_01]: See, well, first thing I would point out, with regard to this, is that when it comes to base of nothingness and similar descriptions, people immediately assume, and that is thanks to the commentaries in contemporary Buddhism, people assume it's a meditative attainment, it's some sort of

185
00:17:01,977 --> 00:17:03,599
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a blanked-out, spaced-out state.

186
00:17:04,580 --> 00:17:09,925
[SPEAKER_01]: But as you can have just a little perspective, it's quite clear that it's nothing to do with that.

187
00:17:09,985 --> 00:17:13,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, he said, it is also from wisdom upon that state.

188
00:17:14,570 --> 00:17:15,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you actually understand it.

189
00:17:17,113 --> 00:17:17,954
[SPEAKER_01]: You discern it.

190
00:17:19,818 --> 00:17:25,341
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not a meditative attainment in a conventional sense of that word.

191
00:17:25,501 --> 00:17:25,801
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

192
00:17:25,861 --> 00:17:28,102
[SPEAKER_01]: You can call that meditation, but that would be the right meditation.

193
00:17:28,522 --> 00:17:32,864
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, another thing I would point out, the paragraph starts with the Noble Disciple.

194
00:17:33,064 --> 00:17:34,465
[SPEAKER_01]: The right view has to be there.

195
00:17:35,526 --> 00:17:44,550
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the right view is there, even such, again, inverted commas, meditative attainments are actually devoid of the right view and wisdom, because that's what meditation is.

196
00:17:45,275 --> 00:17:48,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Cultivation of the right view that you already have beforehand.

197
00:17:50,537 --> 00:17:57,280
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this particular instance, in terms of the... well, you can... you don't have to go as far as the empathetic perceptions.

198
00:17:57,340 --> 00:17:59,962
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, you recognize the nature of perceptions.

199
00:18:00,062 --> 00:18:00,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where it starts.

200
00:18:01,262 --> 00:18:05,264
[SPEAKER_01]: All the central, the coarsest, central perceptions.

201
00:18:05,284 --> 00:18:09,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Here, in the future lives, then there are the form, the perceptions of form.

202
00:18:11,026 --> 00:18:14,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Here, in the future lives, then even the most peaceful perceptions.

203
00:18:15,542 --> 00:18:26,027
[SPEAKER_01]: In the imperturbable, regardless of the diversity of the form, the perceptions of the imperturbable means basically what you discern through the nature of perception, because that's it.

204
00:18:26,527 --> 00:18:31,209
[SPEAKER_01]: What's in common to essential perceptions, to perceptions of form, to the imperturbable perceptions?

205
00:18:31,409 --> 00:18:32,970
[SPEAKER_01]: As it says, they're all perceptions.

206
00:18:33,430 --> 00:18:42,134
[SPEAKER_01]: They all share the nature of perception, sañña, as an aggregate, and which means that discernment can be established on that level.

207
00:18:42,954 --> 00:18:43,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception is there.

208
00:18:45,495 --> 00:18:46,756
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't care what perception.

209
00:18:47,396 --> 00:18:49,617
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't care if it's diversified or unified.

210
00:18:50,177 --> 00:18:52,178
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't care if it's imperturbable or not.

211
00:18:52,899 --> 00:18:53,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception is there.

212
00:18:53,779 --> 00:18:55,060
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is established upon that.

213
00:18:55,520 --> 00:19:00,902
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, through the discernment of the nature of perception, you can see, okay, it has arisen.

214
00:19:01,203 --> 00:19:09,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Even that sublime, refined, imperturbable nature of perception, regardless of the particular manifestations of that perception, that perception has arisen.

215
00:19:11,124 --> 00:19:16,187
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, it ceases, without a reminder, when the time comes, and there is nothing.

216
00:19:17,348 --> 00:19:20,930
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nothingness can... it's sort of perceived in an indirect manner.

217
00:19:21,430 --> 00:19:30,735
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't perceive nothingness directly, but you can certainly establish your mind in regard to it, in an indirect manner, by surmounting the sphere of perception.

218
00:19:31,416 --> 00:19:40,041
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's done through understanding, through, like, through real, correct phenomenology, knowledge of phenomena, knowledge of arisen things.

219
00:19:40,477 --> 00:19:41,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what dhamma is.

220
00:19:42,659 --> 00:19:47,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Not through, like, you know, watching the nostrils and then entering the astral projection of I don't know what.

221
00:19:48,043 --> 00:19:50,044
[SPEAKER_00]: He resolves upon wisdom.

222
00:19:50,084 --> 00:19:51,505
[SPEAKER_00]: He resolves upon wisdom.

223
00:19:51,525 --> 00:19:51,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

224
00:19:51,786 --> 00:19:53,667
[SPEAKER_00]: You said, or he resolves upon... Yeah.

225
00:19:54,127 --> 00:19:56,889
[SPEAKER_00]: But either he wants... wants to his full confidence...

226
00:19:56,909 --> 00:19:59,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Either his full confidence establishes his mind, right there and then.

227
00:19:59,932 --> 00:20:00,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

228
00:20:00,532 --> 00:20:02,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means he's living it and knowing it.

229
00:20:03,154 --> 00:20:03,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

230
00:20:03,534 --> 00:20:05,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Or he fully discerns it and resolves upon wisdom.

231
00:20:06,697 --> 00:20:08,198
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he... he also knows it.

232
00:20:12,078 --> 00:20:15,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's read the other two ways, because it might be wrong.

233
00:20:16,860 --> 00:20:27,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, because a noble disciple goes on to the forest, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty lot, considers thus, this is void of a self, or of what belongs to a self.

234
00:20:27,986 --> 00:20:36,510
[SPEAKER_00]: When he's practicing this way, and frequently abides thus, his mind acquires confidence in this place, and so on.

235
00:20:37,050 --> 00:20:37,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Void off.

236
00:20:38,691 --> 00:20:40,552
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, as I said,

237
00:20:42,324 --> 00:20:46,146
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience, the inner experience, gives priority to the sense of self.

238
00:20:47,547 --> 00:20:58,074
[SPEAKER_01]: But with the right view, and yeah, you go to physically, to an empty room, that it's empty, it's factually empty, and you use that reflection as factual emptiness of this room.

239
00:20:58,975 --> 00:21:05,639
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same mirroring situation of the emptiness of the sense of self in this experience.

240
00:21:07,220 --> 00:21:08,961
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, it's a negative presence.

241
00:21:09,161 --> 00:21:11,763
[SPEAKER_01]: This room is empty of others.

242
00:21:13,458 --> 00:21:16,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, others are present as absences in this room.

243
00:21:16,960 --> 00:21:17,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Others are absent here.

244
00:21:18,721 --> 00:21:22,663
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is this body, so is this mind, and so on, absent of this self?

245
00:21:24,424 --> 00:21:27,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the self is only a negative phenomenon in regard to it.

246
00:21:29,566 --> 00:21:35,950
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you start seeing it as such, you stop regarding it as yours, and you certainly stop regarding it as a center of your experience.

247
00:21:37,907 --> 00:21:39,708
[SPEAKER_00]: You're seeing things like the body...

248
00:21:39,868 --> 00:21:40,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Empty of self.

249
00:21:40,908 --> 00:21:42,329
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not empty of body, it's empty...

250
00:21:42,349 --> 00:21:43,029
[SPEAKER_01]: It's empty of self.

251
00:21:43,389 --> 00:21:45,970
[SPEAKER_00]: It's got a room there, it's got a body, it's feelings there...

252
00:21:45,990 --> 00:21:46,790
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's empty of this.

253
00:21:49,111 --> 00:21:50,571
[SPEAKER_01]: And that doesn't mean that this doesn't exist.

254
00:21:50,912 --> 00:21:52,032
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole thing.

255
00:21:53,472 --> 00:22:00,255
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, if you say, oh, this room is empty of self... No, self exists as an absence, as a thing that's not there.

256
00:22:01,147 --> 00:22:04,829
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's enough for you to stop regarding it as yours and that's all that matters.

257
00:22:05,590 --> 00:22:09,833
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that phenomenon that you've taken to be yourself, it's there, it's just not yours.

258
00:22:11,174 --> 00:22:16,177
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a different way of saying that this experience here is empty of this my-self.

259
00:22:18,638 --> 00:22:21,300
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the self is absent and through that absence I can't own it.

260
00:22:22,601 --> 00:22:24,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, that absence is present.

261
00:22:24,742 --> 00:22:26,604
[SPEAKER_01]: The absence of self is present.

262
00:22:27,323 --> 00:22:29,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you won't be able to designate it or talk about it.

263
00:22:30,185 --> 00:22:35,988
[SPEAKER_01]: But by recognizing it, that it's absent, means, well, it can't be yours, because if it's yours, you'll have it all the time.

264
00:22:37,469 --> 00:22:38,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is an empty sense of self.

265
00:22:40,031 --> 00:22:44,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's enough for you to stop regarding it as belonging to you.

266
00:22:49,836 --> 00:22:51,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he attains the base of nothingness.

267
00:22:51,998 --> 00:22:52,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

268
00:22:52,918 --> 00:22:56,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he attains the base of voidness, isn't it?

269
00:22:58,613 --> 00:23:03,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Once there's full confidence, he either takes it to the base of nothingness now, or else he resolves upon it with wisdom.

270
00:23:03,697 --> 00:23:05,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Through the perception of avoidance.

271
00:23:05,518 --> 00:23:05,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

272
00:23:05,938 --> 00:23:09,481
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, he stops appropriating any perception.

273
00:23:09,841 --> 00:23:11,462
[SPEAKER_01]: This room is empty of self.

274
00:23:11,662 --> 00:23:16,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, through not being able to appropriate your sense of self, there's nothing that belongs to you.

275
00:23:17,326 --> 00:23:18,367
[SPEAKER_01]: You resolve upon that.

276
00:23:19,507 --> 00:23:22,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Means nothing that arises, nothing that manifests, i.e.

277
00:23:22,750 --> 00:23:26,112
[SPEAKER_01]: none of the perceptions that arise, no matter how sublime or diversified,

278
00:23:27,388 --> 00:23:29,031
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be appropriated either.

279
00:23:30,793 --> 00:23:32,115
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's pretty much nothingness.

280
00:23:34,318 --> 00:23:36,221
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't see nothingness in a direct manner.

281
00:23:39,048 --> 00:23:42,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Only by surmounting perception, mind can get established upon nothingness.

282
00:23:42,769 --> 00:23:49,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I always want to... And you can surmount the perception by resolving upon wisdom of, like, over the sphere of perception, outside of the perception.

283
00:23:50,011 --> 00:24:03,415
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, again, it's still, even the resolving upon wisdom, it's still done, like you, you establish yourself upon nothingness in that negative manner, through wisely surmounting the sphere of perception.

284
00:24:05,040 --> 00:24:07,141
[SPEAKER_01]: But it all revolves around appropriation.

285
00:24:07,181 --> 00:24:14,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the first example you gave of sensuality and different perceptions and so on, imperturbable, or the voidness.

286
00:24:15,387 --> 00:24:16,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, voidness.

287
00:24:16,328 --> 00:24:17,368
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, in our case, nothing.

288
00:24:17,789 --> 00:24:18,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing's there.

289
00:24:19,270 --> 00:24:20,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing is there.

290
00:24:23,192 --> 00:24:23,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

291
00:24:24,874 --> 00:24:25,614
[SPEAKER_00]: I was always...

292
00:24:25,754 --> 00:24:27,175
[SPEAKER_00]: I wanted to imagine nothing.

293
00:24:27,896 --> 00:24:28,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the thing.

294
00:24:28,996 --> 00:24:29,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagination

295
00:24:31,982 --> 00:24:44,374
[SPEAKER_01]: If your mind is developed in regard to your, or let's say more correctly, abstraction, some imagination, because it's kind of based on the facts, but you're not really living those facts, thus you're just abstracting the thinking, abstract, abstract thinking.

296
00:24:44,955 --> 00:24:45,656
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't correspond.

297
00:24:45,916 --> 00:24:49,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your mind was to be established upon that abstract thinking, it would cease to be abstract.

298
00:24:51,990 --> 00:24:56,172
[SPEAKER_01]: You will become, oh, a valid thought that has served to establish your mind.

299
00:24:56,712 --> 00:25:01,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Like what we spoke before, you use more general perceptions to abandon more particular, more diversified, more troublesome.

300
00:25:02,535 --> 00:25:10,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Upon the perceptions of the forest, you abandon perceptions of the village, which means whatever disturbance arises on account of perceptions of the village, cannot affect you.

301
00:25:11,339 --> 00:25:16,161
[SPEAKER_01]: But the... any disturbance that arises on account of perceptions of the forest, that can still affect you.

302
00:25:16,181 --> 00:25:17,922
[SPEAKER_01]: So you recognize that, so you go even higher.

303
00:25:19,278 --> 00:25:22,880
[SPEAKER_01]: What is even greater perception than perception of forest and perception of earth?

304
00:25:24,221 --> 00:25:24,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what?

305
00:25:25,101 --> 00:25:27,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, in this case, you can go even higher than that.

306
00:25:27,403 --> 00:25:28,844
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say, well, what is the perception?

307
00:25:29,364 --> 00:25:33,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception of village, perception of forest, perception of earth, they're all perceptions.

308
00:25:35,447 --> 00:25:37,949
[SPEAKER_01]: And perception is... and a reasoned thing has to cease.

309
00:25:38,009 --> 00:25:42,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, whatever is perceived, whatever arises, has to come to an end.

310
00:25:42,932 --> 00:25:43,752
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is...

311
00:25:43,772 --> 00:25:47,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Resulting upon that, it's the experience of nothingness.

312
00:25:47,594 --> 00:25:48,835
[SPEAKER_00]: The perception of nothingness.

313
00:25:49,334 --> 00:25:49,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

314
00:25:50,315 --> 00:25:58,241
[SPEAKER_01]: You can then start discerning the perception of that nothing, so to speak, in regard to the positive perception that you have surmounted.

315
00:25:58,721 --> 00:26:01,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Because obviously there's a surmounting of that perception as well.

316
00:26:02,544 --> 00:26:02,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

317
00:26:03,004 --> 00:26:05,406
[SPEAKER_01]: There is higher than base of nothingness as well.

318
00:26:09,149 --> 00:26:11,491
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a third way to take nothingness.

319
00:26:14,042 --> 00:26:17,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, because the Noble Disciples considers thus, I'm...

320
00:26:17,306 --> 00:26:20,010
[SPEAKER_00]: I am not anything belonging to anyone, anyway.

321
00:26:20,290 --> 00:26:21,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, what did I say just before?

322
00:26:21,512 --> 00:26:23,054
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all about appropriation of belonging.

323
00:26:23,414 --> 00:26:23,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

324
00:26:24,275 --> 00:26:27,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Nor is there anything belonging to me in anyone, anyway.

325
00:26:29,415 --> 00:26:31,776
[SPEAKER_00]: When he practices in this way, it frequently binds us.

326
00:26:31,856 --> 00:26:33,837
[SPEAKER_00]: His mind acquires confidence in this base.

327
00:26:34,518 --> 00:26:38,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Once there's full confidence, he attains to the base of nothingness or results.

328
00:26:38,860 --> 00:26:39,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

329
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:40,981
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the most refined way.

330
00:26:41,562 --> 00:26:48,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it really points out the appropriation, the belonging, taking his mind, which is what all I've been talking about so far.

331
00:26:49,726 --> 00:26:53,108
[SPEAKER_01]: The first one is just sensuality.

332
00:26:54,809 --> 00:26:55,489
[SPEAKER_01]: What was the other one?

333
00:26:55,609 --> 00:26:56,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensual perceptions?

334
00:26:58,629 --> 00:27:01,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Perceptions of form and then perceptions of imperturbable.

335
00:27:01,990 --> 00:27:02,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

336
00:27:03,950 --> 00:27:06,311
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that already implies me and mine.

337
00:27:07,211 --> 00:27:09,372
[SPEAKER_01]: For the second one, it's a bit more personal.

338
00:27:09,452 --> 00:27:15,173
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's, oh, these perceptions here, this room, as this room is void of others, so this is void.

339
00:27:15,553 --> 00:27:23,115
[SPEAKER_01]: But the third one, it's even more pointed out to just that experience of mind, not even necessarily the fully-blown sense of self.

340
00:27:24,303 --> 00:27:32,010
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, whichever you take, if you develop it, it will lead you to that samādhi, to the point of perception of nothingness, being established upon perception of nothingness.

341
00:27:34,012 --> 00:27:39,137
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by having nothing, like nothing here belongs to me, or do I belong to anyone?

342
00:27:39,257 --> 00:27:41,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing in the future will belong to me, or will I belong to...

343
00:27:41,800 --> 00:27:43,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing in the past has belonged to me.

344
00:27:43,922 --> 00:27:45,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So... That's not possible.

345
00:27:45,863 --> 00:27:47,285
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not possible for anything.

346
00:27:47,305 --> 00:27:49,247
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that it didn't belong to me, but it could.

347
00:27:50,136 --> 00:27:52,277
[SPEAKER_01]: When he says nothing in the future, that's what it means.

348
00:27:52,377 --> 00:27:53,718
[SPEAKER_01]: It means a present possibility.

349
00:27:54,418 --> 00:27:56,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nothing in the future will belong to him.

350
00:27:56,699 --> 00:27:58,480
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible for things to belong to him.

351
00:27:58,560 --> 00:28:08,045
[SPEAKER_01]: And by truly permeating your whole experience with that recognition, that ownership is inherently impossible,

352
00:28:09,123 --> 00:28:18,551
[SPEAKER_01]: You surmount the entire sphere of ownership, which is a sphere of perceptions, sphere of things that you can perceive, including abstract, remote, ambiguous thoughts.

353
00:28:18,911 --> 00:28:22,414
[SPEAKER_01]: They appear as such, you can entertain the idea of ownership as mine.

354
00:28:23,154 --> 00:28:36,385
[SPEAKER_01]: But fully cultivating this attitude, eventually it will permeate and saturate your experience entirely, in all of its sort of proliferated branches, so to speak, and that ownership will fade.

355
00:28:37,457 --> 00:28:50,623
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just because you recognize... you recognize that the only reason you were entertaining notions of ownership, it was by not fully, not emotionally understanding that you were not able to own them in the first place.

356
00:28:52,844 --> 00:28:53,824
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you were owning them.

357
00:28:55,745 --> 00:28:59,227
[SPEAKER_01]: You were appropriating them because you didn't know that you actually can't.

358
00:28:59,427 --> 00:29:00,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Appropriation is inconceivable.

359
00:29:02,028 --> 00:29:06,090
[SPEAKER_00]: And the stopping of their pursuits is... Well, it's a result of that.

360
00:29:06,250 --> 00:29:06,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

361
00:29:07,511 --> 00:29:11,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, like, there won't be... well, then pursuit will become equally inconceivable.

362
00:29:14,173 --> 00:29:18,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the entire nature of pursuit was based upon that misperception.

363
00:29:21,135 --> 00:29:21,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

364
00:29:24,636 --> 00:29:33,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it goes on to say that the... the base of neither perception or non-perception is the highest.

365
00:29:35,180 --> 00:29:36,020
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said,

366
00:29:37,352 --> 00:29:39,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Negative... like what I gave the example before.

367
00:29:39,833 --> 00:29:41,033
[SPEAKER_01]: The perceptions of forest.

368
00:29:41,334 --> 00:29:45,675
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're in the village, your mind gets established upon perceptions of the forest.

369
00:29:46,496 --> 00:29:53,758
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you enter the village, that perception, the forest, becomes a negative perception in regard to your current perceptions of the village.

370
00:29:55,239 --> 00:30:05,703
[SPEAKER_01]: But because your mind is still established upon that perception, which is being perceived in a negative manner, nothing bothers you in the village that you can perceive, that you perceive, that you might perceive.

371
00:30:06,908 --> 00:30:15,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you recognize, okay, so I live in the forest, when I'm in the village, if I'm established upon the perception of the forest, I'll be free from disturbance in the village, but not from the disturbance in the forest.

372
00:30:17,110 --> 00:30:24,512
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the negative perception in regard to the forest and the village, further down, it's the perception of earth, the entire earth, the element of earth.

373
00:30:26,172 --> 00:30:30,713
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a negative perception in regard to this more particular perception, or you can say more general perception.

374
00:30:32,274 --> 00:30:34,455
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a negative when you're perceiving other things.

375
00:30:35,035 --> 00:30:35,896
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not there.

376
00:30:35,936 --> 00:30:37,516
[SPEAKER_01]: It's present as an absent perception.

377
00:30:37,596 --> 00:30:38,957
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's ultimately nothingness.

378
00:30:39,437 --> 00:30:41,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Shares that very nature of that very principle.

379
00:30:42,298 --> 00:30:43,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Quiet, Timmy.

380
00:30:43,999 --> 00:30:46,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Oops, sorry.

381
00:30:49,762 --> 00:30:52,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through cultivating the negative perceptions...

382
00:30:52,997 --> 00:30:55,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind gets resolved upon it and establishes in it.

383
00:30:55,818 --> 00:30:58,438
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that is a perception in that negative manner.

384
00:30:58,638 --> 00:31:01,439
[SPEAKER_01]: In that indirect manner, it's still our perception.

385
00:31:01,779 --> 00:31:08,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, neither perception nor no perception would be a state that goes even beyond that perception of nothingness.

386
00:31:10,061 --> 00:31:11,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the absence.

387
00:31:11,861 --> 00:31:12,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the absence.

388
00:31:12,382 --> 00:31:13,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, just general.

389
00:31:13,342 --> 00:31:13,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

390
00:31:20,827 --> 00:31:29,385
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so as this village is absent of any forest and perceptions of forest, so is this entire body.

391
00:31:30,881 --> 00:31:39,105
[SPEAKER_01]: that I'm now using on this particular level, walking through a village, is also absent of any owner, of any self that actually owns it.

392
00:31:39,725 --> 00:31:48,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, more specifically, more precisely, as the third example says, of any belonging to the sense of self, or whatever arises in regard to it.

393
00:31:48,829 --> 00:31:55,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, the sense of self, or rather, that which is taken to be a sense of self, stays there, but it loses its ownership, loses its belonging.

394
00:31:56,032 --> 00:31:57,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's not really a self anymore.

395
00:31:59,015 --> 00:32:01,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not yourself.

396
00:32:09,938 --> 00:32:22,260
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can submerge neither perception nor perception through the entire cessation of perception and feeling.

397
00:32:22,280 --> 00:32:28,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, OK. Because, as I said, when that big... when that big hook clings,

398
00:32:31,816 --> 00:32:55,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because if you assume, if your upādāna is based upon neither perception nor perception, meaning the mind has been developed to such an extent that it can actually discern that state, which means all of the more diversified states have been abandoned and you cannot entertain any notions of ownership in regard to them.

399
00:32:57,022 --> 00:33:08,346
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, when you learn and you get established upon the perceptions of forests and wilderness, the idea of a village, of a crowded place, the mind just will not go there.

400
00:33:10,607 --> 00:33:19,131
[SPEAKER_01]: But imagine if you establish it to such a refined point, a refinement, whereby you surmount the perception in its entirety.

401
00:33:19,611 --> 00:33:20,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that is the sublime.

402
00:33:23,012 --> 00:33:24,373
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, you can assume that.

403
00:33:24,593 --> 00:33:25,033
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be

404
00:33:25,850 --> 00:33:29,451
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be kind of entertaining certain assumptions in regard to that.

405
00:33:29,831 --> 00:33:38,572
[SPEAKER_01]: But that means that all the other assumptions, all of it, not just the coarse ones, even the ones that are regarded relatively non-coarse, have been abandoned.

406
00:33:39,152 --> 00:33:40,853
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that is the place.

407
00:33:40,953 --> 00:33:46,514
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to create something, develop your mind and cling to that freely, because actually there isn't much there to cling on to.

408
00:33:48,574 --> 00:33:53,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, as a result of that, you abandon all the other more dangerous assumptions.

409
00:33:57,815 --> 00:33:58,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

410
00:33:58,115 --> 00:34:00,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Guru says when someone's practicing for nibbāna...

411
00:34:01,176 --> 00:34:02,237
[SPEAKER_01]: They need to abandon that.

412
00:34:02,777 --> 00:34:03,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

413
00:34:03,457 --> 00:34:09,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, because practicing thus, it might not be and it might not be for me.

414
00:34:09,220 --> 00:34:11,180
[SPEAKER_00]: It will not be and it will not be for me.

415
00:34:11,981 --> 00:34:15,362
[SPEAKER_00]: What exists, what has come to be, that's... That's basically...

416
00:34:15,402 --> 00:34:15,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning.

417
00:34:16,042 --> 00:34:16,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

418
00:34:16,743 --> 00:34:19,844
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is developing that reflection.

419
00:34:20,684 --> 00:34:23,886
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not just... because as we said before...

420
00:34:24,126 --> 00:34:25,206
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, he's saying...

421
00:34:25,246 --> 00:34:26,807
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just the fact that you can't know now.

422
00:34:27,613 --> 00:34:33,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Fail is to recognize that even a possibility of ownership cannot occur.

423
00:34:34,936 --> 00:34:45,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you might think, okay, I don't know anything now, but you can still entertain implicitly, or rather tacitly assume within that ambiguous...

424
00:34:45,341 --> 00:34:48,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply assume by not looking at it.

425
00:34:48,062 --> 00:34:55,205
[SPEAKER_01]: You end up assuming that tiny little remote possibility of ownership still might be possible.

426
00:34:56,523 --> 00:35:00,646
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will entertain that by default, simply by not not entertaining it.

427
00:35:01,146 --> 00:35:08,392
[SPEAKER_01]: By not looking at that possibility as impossible, you will automatically entertain the notion of that possibility.

428
00:35:08,492 --> 00:35:11,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the reflection, it might not be.

429
00:35:12,795 --> 00:35:18,519
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it will not even be possible for it to be for me, let alone be for me now.

430
00:35:19,039 --> 00:35:22,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you really pervade, so to speak,

431
00:35:23,438 --> 00:35:29,322
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole sphere of nature of possibility, which is much subtler than anything that isn't present in front of you.

432
00:35:30,283 --> 00:35:34,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have this present instinct, I don't own anything, but you still entertain the possibility of ownership.

433
00:35:36,387 --> 00:35:39,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you never give in to it, you might entertain it for the rest of your life.

434
00:35:40,089 --> 00:35:47,054
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want to practice for nibbāna, an ultimate relinquishment, you start really targeting, so to speak, those possibilities.

435
00:35:47,952 --> 00:35:52,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, okay, it's obvious I don't own anything in front of me, around me, anything in the past.

436
00:35:52,934 --> 00:35:53,974
[SPEAKER_01]: See, I couldn't have kept it.

437
00:35:54,254 --> 00:36:03,677
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can still cherish a tiny little notion of... just the possibility of ownership as an abstract idea, even that.

438
00:36:04,217 --> 00:36:09,499
[SPEAKER_01]: But that will be the point that you're touching, the point that you're interfering with, the point that you didn't let go of.

439
00:36:12,235 --> 00:36:14,516
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you practice, it will not be.

440
00:36:15,016 --> 00:36:16,376
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be for me.

441
00:36:16,937 --> 00:36:19,358
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... it's impossible for it to be for me.

442
00:36:19,518 --> 00:36:20,478
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just inconceivable.

443
00:36:20,498 --> 00:36:31,502
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what, when, in the future, how far, how long in the future, it's just... it's impossible for this domain, the touch, to come together and establish belonging.

444
00:36:31,822 --> 00:36:33,002
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

445
00:36:33,823 --> 00:36:40,525
[SPEAKER_01]: And you practice that long enough, presently, right here, right now, in regard to the future possibilities, and, yeah, that is nibbāna.

446
00:36:51,192 --> 00:36:55,193
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess we have, again, this moment of the gap, impossibilities.

447
00:36:56,553 --> 00:36:57,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

448
00:36:58,614 --> 00:37:02,915
[SPEAKER_01]: And as you can see, that gap can obviously stretch to very refined levels.

449
00:37:02,935 --> 00:37:08,316
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you can, you can surmount, and even Pratyusha can surmount the gap of sensual craving.

450
00:37:10,516 --> 00:37:18,298
[SPEAKER_01]: But the gap of the future ownership, like the possibility of ownership, can still be very much maintained.

451
00:37:22,435 --> 00:37:32,764
[SPEAKER_01]: But by recognizing that that gap is insurmountable, that it's impossible to fill, impossible to bridge, you stop trying to build a bridge.

452
00:37:34,185 --> 00:37:38,950
[SPEAKER_01]: You will keep trying to build a bridge for as long as you think it's possible for it to work, it's possible to build it.

453
00:37:40,151 --> 00:37:45,415
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove it, or rather you remove the hope, the notion of that possibility.

454
00:37:45,435 --> 00:37:48,338
[SPEAKER_01]: You fully understand it as, oh no, this is impossible.

455
00:37:49,556 --> 00:37:54,400
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, all of the efforts to build a bridge just... you don't have to then go... you automatically... they stop.

456
00:37:54,560 --> 00:37:55,181
[SPEAKER_01]: They disappear.

457
00:37:56,342 --> 00:37:59,024
[SPEAKER_00]: But all... the key thing is all the suffering ends.

458
00:37:59,364 --> 00:37:59,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

459
00:37:59,885 --> 00:38:00,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yeah.

460
00:38:00,745 --> 00:38:00,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Then.

461
00:38:01,786 --> 00:38:04,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the result of the suffering was craving for the bridge.

462
00:38:04,409 --> 00:38:04,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

463
00:38:05,455 --> 00:38:09,399
[SPEAKER_00]: And you think that the bridge will get rid of all the suffering, but in fact...

464
00:38:09,559 --> 00:38:22,611
[SPEAKER_01]: But you were suffering because you were basically trying to build that bridge, trying to gap, trying to surmount, trying to cross, which is impossible, trying to connect that which is, well, inconnectable.

465
00:38:22,791 --> 00:38:23,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

466
00:38:34,233 --> 00:38:44,102
[SPEAKER_01]: And as you can see, simply through thinking that it is possible to build a bridge over that gap of your existence, you appropriate it.

467
00:38:44,703 --> 00:38:45,964
[SPEAKER_01]: You appropriate the other shore.

468
00:38:46,825 --> 00:38:49,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You appropriate the possibility of being on the other side.

469
00:38:50,137 --> 00:38:58,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Where that bridge is built and the whole thing has been filled and connected and unified.

470
00:38:59,221 --> 00:39:06,324
[SPEAKER_01]: But through seeing that this is impossible, you stop entertaining the notions of the other side of the gap, of ownership.

471
00:39:07,425 --> 00:39:12,547
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see, through ownership, you maintain the notion of crossing, of building a bridge.

472
00:39:13,608 --> 00:39:17,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, through the efforts of building a bridge, through the assumption that it's possible to fill that gap,

473
00:39:19,601 --> 00:39:26,108
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep assuming the ownership of the result of filling that gap, of that other side that you can close.

474
00:39:28,643 --> 00:39:43,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't necessarily try here and now, even in practicing restraint, the fact that you're maintaining the notion that it is possible to connect with that other side of that gap, means you are still very much within that assumption.

475
00:39:43,274 --> 00:39:44,195
[SPEAKER_01]: You're choosing not to.

476
00:39:44,275 --> 00:39:53,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're choosing not to, yeah, you still own the other shore, through the possibility of, oh, I can cross there, through building this bridge over this insurmountable gap.

477
00:39:53,442 --> 00:39:53,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

478
00:39:54,002 --> 00:39:57,243
[SPEAKER_01]: You fully understand that in Samantabhadra, that was... that is peace.

479
00:39:57,303 --> 00:40:00,024
[SPEAKER_01]: The peace was not unifying broken pieces or whatever else.

480
00:40:00,364 --> 00:40:01,744
[SPEAKER_01]: The peace was abandoning them all.

481
00:40:03,244 --> 00:40:12,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you have broken pieces of a vase, for as long as you hold on to deep perceptions of a vase, those pieces will be... well, will be valuable, will be useful, will be...

482
00:40:13,876 --> 00:40:22,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Object of your effort to bring back the vows, to build it up as it was before, or whatever notions you might entertain, or emotional attachment towards it.

483
00:40:22,962 --> 00:40:27,284
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say, I don't care about it anymore, all those pieces become literally worthless.

484
00:40:28,285 --> 00:40:28,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Entirely.

485
00:40:29,946 --> 00:40:32,987
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you... and especially when you recognize that you can't have the vows.

486
00:40:33,528 --> 00:40:34,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

487
00:40:34,248 --> 00:40:35,589
[SPEAKER_01]: And, well, you couldn't have had it.

488
00:40:36,430 --> 00:40:40,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it was truly your vows all the time, you wouldn't have been broken in the first place.

489
00:40:42,049 --> 00:40:43,370
[SPEAKER_01]: You have been in full control of it.

490
00:40:43,450 --> 00:40:44,771
[SPEAKER_01]: That means it's independent of you.

491
00:40:45,351 --> 00:40:50,494
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, this gap that you're trying to surmount by pursuing your desires, it's independent of you.

492
00:40:50,534 --> 00:40:51,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's insurmountable.

493
00:40:52,355 --> 00:41:01,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, only through the notions of surmounting it, you are, yourself, fully responsible for maintaining the constant presence of the desire.

494
00:41:02,100 --> 00:41:05,222
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop maintaining that, the desire fades, because it needs maintenance.

495
00:41:05,862 --> 00:41:08,664
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Nibbīla said, the object of desire is more desire.

496
00:41:09,664 --> 00:41:09,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

497
00:41:11,737 --> 00:41:19,305
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sole purpose of desire is the presence of desire.

498
00:41:23,009 --> 00:41:26,593
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see that, because desire kind of tricks you by projecting a look.

499
00:41:27,154 --> 00:41:28,435
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you're desiring for.

500
00:41:28,636 --> 00:41:32,400
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't see the nature of desire, because you're focused on a particular manifestation of it.

501
00:41:32,995 --> 00:41:34,756
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, a desire is already fulfilled.

502
00:41:35,296 --> 00:41:36,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

503
00:41:36,236 --> 00:41:38,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire is fulfilled by itself, by being a desire.

504
00:41:39,318 --> 00:41:40,138
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine by itself.

505
00:41:40,238 --> 00:41:44,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Only through the discrepancy, which you then assume, feels incomplete.

506
00:41:45,560 --> 00:41:46,781
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're fully responsible for that.

507
00:41:48,581 --> 00:41:49,842
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know how people...

508
00:41:50,962 --> 00:41:55,944
[SPEAKER_01]: The notion is, there's a desire, and then obviously there's a projected discrepancy of a fulfillment of desire.

509
00:41:55,984 --> 00:41:58,285
[SPEAKER_01]: If I do this, and this, and this, my desire will be fulfilled.

510
00:41:58,305 --> 00:41:59,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you crave for that.

511
00:42:00,001 --> 00:42:02,926
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you entertain, that's how you maintain the notion of desire.

512
00:42:03,627 --> 00:42:12,101
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could equally, so to speak, fulfill your desire by forgetting about your desire.

513
00:42:14,406 --> 00:42:17,508
[SPEAKER_01]: So you feel like, oh, if I pursue my desire, I fulfill my desire.

514
00:42:18,148 --> 00:42:24,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Equal to that pursuit, you could equally do that, just forget about your desire, and your desire will be equally fulfilled.

515
00:42:24,653 --> 00:42:26,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it was fulfilled to begin with.

516
00:42:26,794 --> 00:42:32,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire in itself, it's a valid phenomenon, a reason as such, perfectly sufficient by itself, in itself.

517
00:42:34,079 --> 00:42:39,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But the discrepancy created is in regard to another projected scenario, that it's incomplete in regard to this.

518
00:42:40,363 --> 00:42:41,964
[SPEAKER_01]: But in itself, it's quite complete.

519
00:42:42,911 --> 00:42:47,676
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you were to forget about the scenario, the discrepancy is forgotten about.

520
00:42:47,977 --> 00:42:50,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, desire is completely hurt.

521
00:42:51,080 --> 00:42:51,500
[SPEAKER_00]: It ceases.

522
00:42:51,601 --> 00:42:51,901
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gone.

523
00:42:51,941 --> 00:42:53,863
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't be just forgetful.

524
00:42:53,943 --> 00:42:54,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

525
00:42:54,404 --> 00:42:56,726
[SPEAKER_01]: What I mean by forgetting about desire is restraint.

526
00:42:57,207 --> 00:42:57,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

527
00:42:59,890 --> 00:43:03,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by restraining, you stop pursuing it and then it falls off and you start thinking about something else.

528
00:43:04,355 --> 00:43:04,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

529
00:43:07,862 --> 00:43:13,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if you can selectively forget only about desires, but don't forget about your forgetting, and so on.

530
00:43:15,545 --> 00:43:18,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you fulfill all your desires by restraints.

531
00:43:18,726 --> 00:43:21,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, you would.

532
00:43:22,168 --> 00:43:23,188
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be true fulfillment.

533
00:43:23,228 --> 00:43:27,850
[SPEAKER_01]: It will bring all desires to an end, to a cessation.

534
00:43:28,970 --> 00:43:32,191
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cure your itch by not itching, by not scratching also.

535
00:43:33,992 --> 00:43:35,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Because scratching makes making you worse.

536
00:43:36,732 --> 00:43:37,352
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that simple.

537
00:43:39,093 --> 00:43:42,814
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just most people are not strong enough to persist in non-scratching.

538
00:43:48,317 --> 00:43:50,778
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason they're not strong enough is because they don't practice.

539
00:43:51,438 --> 00:43:52,318
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't develop strength.

540
00:43:53,079 --> 00:43:57,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, they're fully responsible for being weak, for being unable to restrain.

541
00:44:01,122 --> 00:44:04,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, they're fully responsible for being pulled by their desire.

542
00:44:06,473 --> 00:44:06,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I was...

543
00:44:07,534 --> 00:44:10,318
[SPEAKER_00]: I was going to the beginning of that sutta, in nature.

544
00:44:10,658 --> 00:44:11,099
[SPEAKER_00]: So, fine.

545
00:44:13,021 --> 00:44:19,930
[SPEAKER_00]: And we'll start off with Bhikkhu's essential pleasures are impermanent, hollow, false, deceptive.

546
00:44:20,251 --> 00:44:21,272
[SPEAKER_00]: They are illusory.

547
00:44:22,313 --> 00:44:23,475
[SPEAKER_00]: The prattle of fools.

548
00:44:25,322 --> 00:44:28,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Essential pleasures here and now, and essential pleasures in life to come.

549
00:44:28,284 --> 00:44:32,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Essential pleasures... perceptions here and now, and essential perceptions in life to come.

550
00:44:33,248 --> 00:44:39,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Both alike are Māra's realm, Māra's domain, Māra's bait, Māra's hunting ground.

551
00:44:39,653 --> 00:44:47,439
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of them, these evil, unwholesome mental states such as covetousness, ill-will, and presumption arise.

552
00:44:47,840 --> 00:44:52,103
[SPEAKER_00]: And they constitute an obstruction to a noble disciple in training here.

553
00:44:53,244 --> 00:44:54,445
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what presumption is.

554
00:44:55,966 --> 00:44:58,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know what a part of the word is that he translates right now.

555
00:44:58,568 --> 00:44:59,849
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

556
00:44:59,869 --> 00:45:00,970
[SPEAKER_01]: You get the context from the other.

557
00:45:02,211 --> 00:45:18,945
[SPEAKER_00]: But when he, yeah, when a bhikkhu considers our sensual pleasures here and now, and so on, constitute an obstruction in the training, suppose I were to abide with the mind abundant and exalted, having transcended the world and made a firm determination with the mind.

558
00:45:19,405 --> 00:45:22,588
[SPEAKER_00]: When I do so, there will be no more evil, unwholesome mental states.

559
00:45:24,049 --> 00:45:24,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

560
00:45:25,747 --> 00:45:32,229
[SPEAKER_00]: And with the abandoning of that, my mind will be unlimited, immeasurable and well-developed.

561
00:45:32,929 --> 00:45:38,391
[SPEAKER_00]: When he practices in this way and frequently abides thus, his mind acquires confidence in the space... And there becomes the norm.

562
00:45:38,631 --> 00:45:41,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the inter... inter... interval.

563
00:45:43,432 --> 00:45:44,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it requires development.

564
00:45:45,473 --> 00:45:50,554
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how, from abstraction, it's brought down to the actuality.

565
00:45:52,279 --> 00:46:01,788
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do it the first time, oh, this is abstract, but if you actually start pondering, discerning, establishing, understanding upon it, and then you abide in it frequently enough, it becomes a norm.

566
00:46:02,789 --> 00:46:04,691
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes a state that your mind is attained.

567
00:46:05,671 --> 00:46:05,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh.

568
00:46:07,793 --> 00:46:12,237
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, as he says, it is Mara's... Mara's fear.

569
00:46:12,598 --> 00:46:13,379
[SPEAKER_00]: It's Mara's stuff.

570
00:46:14,363 --> 00:46:15,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Those perceptions.

571
00:46:15,543 --> 00:46:19,524
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is... And what does it mean when saying this is Mara's?

572
00:46:19,784 --> 00:46:20,684
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it's not yours.

573
00:46:20,804 --> 00:46:21,825
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it cannot be yours.

574
00:46:21,845 --> 00:46:22,925
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it's owned by another.

575
00:46:24,065 --> 00:46:24,565
[SPEAKER_01]: By Mara.

576
00:46:25,505 --> 00:46:26,365
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what they said.

577
00:46:26,625 --> 00:46:29,746
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because people take that which belongs to Mara.

578
00:46:30,206 --> 00:46:31,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Mara does with them what he wants.

579
00:46:32,307 --> 00:46:33,047
[SPEAKER_01]: What do they take?

580
00:46:33,987 --> 00:46:35,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, what is it that belongs to Mara?

581
00:46:35,987 --> 00:46:37,428
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, I belongs to Mara.

582
00:46:37,768 --> 00:46:39,048
[SPEAKER_01]: iPhones belong to Mara.

583
00:46:39,951 --> 00:46:43,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Hearer belongs to mārā, sounds belong to mārā.

584
00:46:44,113 --> 00:46:47,474
[SPEAKER_01]: All the perceptions in its entirety belongs to mārā.

585
00:46:47,594 --> 00:46:55,578
[SPEAKER_00]: But wouldn't it be somehow saying that there is a self and it's... there is a... No, there is a self, but it's not yours.

586
00:46:55,658 --> 00:46:56,498
[SPEAKER_00]: It's mārā's.

587
00:46:56,678 --> 00:46:57,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Mārā is the ultimate self.

588
00:47:02,020 --> 00:47:03,861
[SPEAKER_01]: You just have to stop regarding it as mine.

589
00:47:04,461 --> 00:47:07,823
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as this very thing says, stop regarding

590
00:47:09,097 --> 00:47:10,437
[SPEAKER_01]: That it is possible to be yours.

591
00:47:11,137 --> 00:47:13,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're not taking it, it might be possible.

592
00:47:14,398 --> 00:47:17,038
[SPEAKER_01]: You should start thinking, no, it will not be possible.

593
00:47:17,378 --> 00:47:18,219
[SPEAKER_00]: It is not possible.

594
00:47:18,359 --> 00:47:19,519
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be possible.

595
00:47:21,219 --> 00:47:28,820
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever the Buddha talks about the future, then, in this sutta, he says, and it will not be for me in the future, sensual pleasures here and now, sensual pleasures to come.

596
00:47:29,901 --> 00:47:30,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

597
00:47:31,321 --> 00:47:32,921
[SPEAKER_01]: It's possibility of sensual pleasures.

598
00:47:33,921 --> 00:47:37,822
[SPEAKER_01]: It's equally impossible to be mine, as much as these present sensual pleasures are.

599
00:47:39,188 --> 00:47:41,589
[SPEAKER_01]: And through abandoning that possibility, you actually abandon the present.

600
00:47:42,130 --> 00:47:42,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the other way around.

601
00:47:43,931 --> 00:47:47,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Because possibilities are subtler than there is in actual aspects.

602
00:47:51,955 --> 00:47:52,495
[SPEAKER_01]: More general.

603
00:47:52,515 --> 00:47:53,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Possibilities are more general.

604
00:47:53,576 --> 00:47:54,977
[SPEAKER_01]: They're on the more general level.

605
00:48:06,983 --> 00:48:08,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's...

606
00:48:08,994 --> 00:48:16,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, the sense of self in regard to your five senses, in regard to experience as a whole, it's appropriate.

607
00:48:16,876 --> 00:48:18,556
[SPEAKER_01]: It's regard dear and personal.

608
00:48:19,216 --> 00:48:19,756
[SPEAKER_01]: It's welcomed.

609
00:48:20,416 --> 00:48:21,036
[SPEAKER_01]: It's cherished.

610
00:48:21,696 --> 00:48:22,316
[SPEAKER_01]: It's protected.

611
00:48:23,357 --> 00:48:24,117
[SPEAKER_01]: You do the opposite.

612
00:48:25,117 --> 00:48:25,797
[SPEAKER_01]: You let go of it.

613
00:48:25,877 --> 00:48:26,717
[SPEAKER_01]: You dismantle it.

614
00:48:26,957 --> 00:48:28,738
[SPEAKER_01]: You see that it's impossible to own it.

615
00:48:29,278 --> 00:48:36,279
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that very sense of self that's right here, right now, in the center of your experience, no matter how vague it is, that sense of self, that's mine.

616
00:48:37,738 --> 00:48:38,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Right here, in your core.

617
00:48:40,780 --> 00:48:41,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Which wasn't your core.

618
00:48:43,021 --> 00:48:43,661
[SPEAKER_01]: It was his core.

619
00:48:43,981 --> 00:48:44,842
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was his all the time.

620
00:48:50,005 --> 00:48:51,626
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why monks say, ah, Mahārāja, I see you.

621
00:48:51,966 --> 00:48:52,386
[SPEAKER_01]: I see you.

622
00:48:52,506 --> 00:48:53,186
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing this.

623
00:48:53,307 --> 00:48:53,847
[SPEAKER_01]: I can see you.

624
00:48:55,128 --> 00:48:59,110
[SPEAKER_01]: They see themselves thinking that, but they know, because that's not appropriate anymore.

625
00:48:59,490 --> 00:48:59,990
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the Mahārāja.

626
00:49:00,430 --> 00:49:01,391
[SPEAKER_01]: That self is the Mahārāja.

627
00:49:01,822 --> 00:49:02,803
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is his control.

628
00:49:02,843 --> 00:49:03,604
[SPEAKER_01]: That is his domain.

629
00:49:03,624 --> 00:49:04,945
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he controls all the beings.

630
00:49:05,265 --> 00:49:06,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're appropriate.

631
00:49:06,907 --> 00:49:07,667
[SPEAKER_01]: That which is his.

632
00:49:07,927 --> 00:49:08,628
[SPEAKER_01]: That which is him.

633
00:49:16,095 --> 00:49:20,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is Māra just another way of saying ignorance, religion?

634
00:49:20,378 --> 00:49:21,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, definitely.

635
00:49:21,559 --> 00:49:22,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

636
00:49:24,182 --> 00:49:25,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Of Egypt personified.

637
00:49:26,409 --> 00:49:26,909
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh...

638
00:49:27,729 --> 00:49:29,230
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't... See, that's the thing.

639
00:49:29,250 --> 00:49:35,252
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're personified, but you don't... you can't get rid of that personification, because there will be appropriation of it.

640
00:49:35,972 --> 00:49:44,855
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to truly be able to get rid of the personality, so to speak, means, well, there would be a person.

641
00:49:45,115 --> 00:49:48,296
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be in control of it, but you can't.

642
00:49:48,316 --> 00:49:50,917
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all we can do is stop regarding it as yours.

643
00:49:51,798 --> 00:49:53,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is actually true.

644
00:49:53,618 --> 00:49:55,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Get rid of... getting rid of...

645
00:49:57,057 --> 00:50:06,564
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when that personality, if it's disowned, cannot be owned, fully understood, discerned, that you cannot possibly own it, that's when it just becomes manifested individuality.

646
00:50:07,225 --> 00:50:19,034
[SPEAKER_01]: All these five aggregates arising in certain manners, based on certain habits, certain dried-up notions, or whatever else, but no more, no more abidjan, certainly no more person as in belonging to you.

647
00:50:21,711 --> 00:50:24,475
[SPEAKER_01]: This does not belong to me, nor to anyone.

648
00:50:25,215 --> 00:50:27,698
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible for it to belong.

649
00:50:29,240 --> 00:50:31,002
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Mara still comes to the Buddha.

650
00:50:31,322 --> 00:50:32,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

651
00:50:32,344 --> 00:50:33,625
[SPEAKER_01]: That is Mara's domain.

652
00:50:34,946 --> 00:50:35,787
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Buddha is not there.

653
00:50:35,807 --> 00:50:36,688
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Mara can't see him.

654
00:50:37,650 --> 00:50:39,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Or Mara can't see an Arahant.

655
00:50:41,007 --> 00:50:42,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's just not there anymore, ever.

656
00:50:43,708 --> 00:50:46,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Even as a possibility, Arahant is not there anymore.

657
00:50:46,690 --> 00:50:53,733
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible for him to be there anymore, where Mahārāja can see him, where that personality can can take a grip of him again.

658
00:50:53,833 --> 00:50:54,413
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

659
00:50:54,473 --> 00:50:55,934
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is well beyond it.

660
00:51:06,119 --> 00:51:07,339
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite straightforward, in a way.

661
00:51:07,499 --> 00:51:09,080
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind is developed,

662
00:51:09,788 --> 00:51:18,269
[SPEAKER_01]: To the proper discernment of every phenomenon, be it a thought, a feeling, a perception of this kind, of that kind, it's a reason.

663
00:51:19,070 --> 00:51:24,030
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start training like that, well, all of these things will kind of fit into place.

664
00:51:29,931 --> 00:51:35,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, they have nothing to do with watching your nostrils and entering the mystical absorption states.

665
00:51:36,693 --> 00:51:38,693
[SPEAKER_01]: It is all upon wisdom.

666
00:51:43,417 --> 00:51:45,119
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is all upon it, with wisdom.

667
00:51:45,399 --> 00:51:45,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

668
00:51:54,828 --> 00:51:55,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Come here, boy.

669
00:51:57,550 --> 00:51:57,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Come here, boy.

670
00:52:00,413 --> 00:52:00,873
[SPEAKER_00]: I like it.

671
00:52:00,973 --> 00:52:02,515
[SPEAKER_00]: I like the way the Buddha ended.

672
00:52:06,117 --> 00:52:11,343
[SPEAKER_00]: When he says this, he says, meditate on under, do not delay, or else you will regret it later.

673
00:52:11,903 --> 00:52:12,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

674
00:52:12,704 --> 00:52:14,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no reason to delay.

675
00:52:15,387 --> 00:52:16,868
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't own it right here, right now.

676
00:52:16,928 --> 00:52:20,311
[SPEAKER_01]: And that possibility of future ownership is present right here, right now.

677
00:52:23,134 --> 00:52:25,957
[SPEAKER_01]: So, delaying it means obstructing it unnecessarily.

678
00:52:30,060 --> 00:52:30,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't do it.

679
00:52:31,881 --> 00:52:32,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Really, don't delay.

680
00:52:32,642 --> 00:52:34,123
[SPEAKER_01]: What for?

681
00:52:34,203 --> 00:52:42,449
[SPEAKER_01]: The delay would come from that misperception, that, how do you want to say, in regards to sensuality, distortion.

682
00:52:44,690 --> 00:52:45,671
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where delay comes from.

683
00:52:45,731 --> 00:52:47,032
[SPEAKER_01]: It wants to maintain itself.

684
00:52:47,132 --> 00:52:48,393
[SPEAKER_01]: The mother wants to maintain it.

685
00:52:48,453 --> 00:52:49,333
[SPEAKER_01]: It needs control.

686
00:52:51,675 --> 00:52:52,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, here and now.

687
00:52:59,594 --> 00:53:04,239
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people think their thoughts are abstract, but it's actually the other way around.

688
00:53:07,423 --> 00:53:11,608
[SPEAKER_01]: It's experience, present experience right here, that it's abstract in regard to thoughts.

689
00:53:12,529 --> 00:53:13,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts come first.

690
00:53:13,610 --> 00:53:14,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind comes first.

691
00:53:15,906 --> 00:53:24,433
[SPEAKER_01]: But because, as I said before, because your experience, well, experience of patujana is so centered around the sense of self, that is the center of the universe.

692
00:53:24,493 --> 00:53:26,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Me and mine, I'm the reference point to everything.

693
00:53:26,714 --> 00:53:27,315
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the real.

694
00:53:27,675 --> 00:53:28,236
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the real.

695
00:53:28,276 --> 00:53:33,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're always bound with the senses, with the sensual, with the material aspects of things and so on.

696
00:53:33,680 --> 00:53:34,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Means that becomes your norm.

697
00:53:34,861 --> 00:53:40,025
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, even the most ordinary thoughts are regarded as, oh, this is just abstract.

698
00:53:40,145 --> 00:53:42,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Because every thought seems abstract,

699
00:53:42,967 --> 00:53:43,087
[SPEAKER_01]: i.e.

700
00:53:43,247 --> 00:53:48,770
[SPEAKER_01]: abstracted from your current experience that you're used to, thoughts seem almost unreal.

701
00:53:48,850 --> 00:53:50,511
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why many people think thoughts are not real.

702
00:53:50,591 --> 00:53:51,432
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just in my head.

703
00:53:52,492 --> 00:54:06,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they got so bogged down in the sensual aspect of their experience, their minds are so down in that sort of domain, that sort of lowly sphere, that even the mere thinking feels abstract.

704
00:54:06,640 --> 00:54:09,281
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's only because they're so used to this

705
00:54:10,813 --> 00:54:16,215
[SPEAKER_01]: sphere of sensual, sphere of senses, and domain of senses, and control of senses.

706
00:54:16,275 --> 00:54:27,640
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody is to point out to you that that's the wrong way around, to turn the picture upside down, that which is first, you were putting second, well, somebody corrects that, then you reckon, no, thoughts are the real, thoughts are the center,

707
00:54:28,607 --> 00:54:30,028
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of thoughts...

708
00:54:30,048 --> 00:54:32,930
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mean just thinking any sort of nonsense in regard to your senses.

709
00:54:33,431 --> 00:54:38,675
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the nature of thoughts is the is the center of experience and it's your experience that you're living.

710
00:54:38,775 --> 00:54:41,037
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the abstraction from that.

711
00:54:43,999 --> 00:54:57,690
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start... if you stop doing... if you stop maintaining that abstraction, if you acknowledge the nature of thoughts as present things that you could just... that you just designated, you will stop living that discrepancy that... which is abstraction.

712
00:54:58,590 --> 00:55:11,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, by basically, by freeing yourself from the notions of abstraction in regard to your own experience, you will be suffering less, because there will be less discrepancy created through such notions.

713
00:55:15,195 --> 00:55:17,295
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for Pradyumna, thoughts are real.

714
00:55:18,356 --> 00:55:23,417
[SPEAKER_01]: The rest of his experience is an abstraction, is a big mistake.

715
00:55:23,537 --> 00:55:26,118
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a distortion, as the Buddha said, in terms of sensuality.

716
00:55:27,841 --> 00:55:33,582
[SPEAKER_01]: And because they're so bound with that distortion, they think that's the real thing.

717
00:55:33,602 --> 00:55:38,603
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that which is... doesn't match it, they regard as, oh, this is abstracted from it.

718
00:55:38,843 --> 00:55:44,564
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I have the thought of nothingness, thought of impermeable, a thought, it's just a thought.

719
00:55:45,364 --> 00:55:52,886
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, if you dismiss it as just a thought, well, as I said before, you don't just shoot yourself in a foot, you shoot yourself in the head.

720
00:55:54,096 --> 00:56:04,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you seriously change those notions, because you'll never then recognize the nature of thoughts, which means you'll never then recognize the nature of your mind, because mind is thoughts, dhammas.

721
00:56:05,640 --> 00:56:15,423
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, dhamma, the capital D, is the knowledge of dhammas, like the small d. It's the phenomenology, the knowledge of phenomena.

722
00:56:17,844 --> 00:56:22,365
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you dismiss anything that appears in your mind as just an abstraction,

723
00:56:24,329 --> 00:56:30,533
[SPEAKER_01]: You never look at it, which means you remain bogged down in sensuality, which is what you have at the time of dismissal.

724
00:56:32,093 --> 00:56:32,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

725
00:56:32,854 --> 00:56:36,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you dismiss any helping hand, you're just gonna stay drowning.

726
00:56:41,719 --> 00:56:52,425
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you have notions of the nothingness, what we spoke about, that feels abstract in regard to where your mind is, because your mind hasn't been developed.

727
00:56:52,445 --> 00:56:53,926
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't dismiss it on that account.

728
00:56:54,761 --> 00:56:57,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Just start developing it in regard to that which feels abstract.

729
00:57:00,643 --> 00:57:07,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the more you develop it, the more you'll know about it, the less of that discrepancy will be maintained, which means the less of the abstraction you'll be living.

730
00:57:09,387 --> 00:57:15,610
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you will understand, and you will be able to think nothingness right away, and have your mind set upon it.

731
00:57:18,351 --> 00:57:21,152
[SPEAKER_01]: And as I said with the alternative,

732
00:57:22,317 --> 00:57:27,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Option is, depending on person's faculties, he resolves upon it with wisdom.

733
00:57:28,718 --> 00:57:44,342
[SPEAKER_01]: That means he doesn't necessarily need to develop it and make it actual to the full extent, because he has such a precise wisdom, that when people would think abstractly, he would think correctly.

734
00:57:44,362 --> 00:57:46,642
[SPEAKER_01]: What he would think, the person with the old wisdom,

735
00:57:46,992 --> 00:57:51,596
[SPEAKER_01]: would stand for that which nothingness is, and he would know that, even without actually being there.

736
00:57:51,976 --> 00:57:55,099
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he resolves upon that with his wisdom.

737
00:57:55,399 --> 00:57:56,920
[SPEAKER_01]: He resolves, it is that.

738
00:57:57,060 --> 00:57:58,381
[SPEAKER_01]: That is what nothingness is.

739
00:57:58,501 --> 00:58:07,649
[SPEAKER_01]: And he resolves upon it, and because that is what nothingness really is, he gets results of freedom that would come by being established upon that nothingness.

740
00:58:08,409 --> 00:58:11,271
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's pañña vibhuti, liberated by wisdom.

741
00:58:11,992 --> 00:58:15,655
[SPEAKER_01]: He resolves upon it with wisdom, stays really long enough, all the fetters fall off.

742
00:58:18,103 --> 00:58:18,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have the other way.

743
00:58:18,883 --> 00:58:21,144
[SPEAKER_01]: You liberate the ego also, in both ways.

744
00:58:21,164 --> 00:58:23,945
[SPEAKER_01]: You experience who touches it with the body, as the suttas will describe.

745
00:58:24,705 --> 00:58:28,446
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when that discrepancy between living body and the senses... Oh, full faith.

746
00:58:28,587 --> 00:58:30,687
[SPEAKER_01]: It is fully developed.

747
00:58:30,707 --> 00:58:31,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh.

748
00:58:32,668 --> 00:58:34,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, fully samādhi, fully extinguished.

749
00:58:38,550 --> 00:58:45,932
[SPEAKER_01]: But abstract, it's only abstract if that discrepancy is created by what you're used to, that doesn't match that which you're thinking.

750
00:58:46,553 --> 00:58:47,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you remember what?

751
00:58:47,353 --> 00:58:47,693
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

752
00:58:48,613 --> 00:58:48,993
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

753
00:58:50,675 --> 00:58:51,315
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it, boy?

754
00:58:52,936 --> 00:58:54,297
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we can't go for a walk now.

755
00:58:55,178 --> 00:59:18,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh

756
00:59:19,771 --> 00:59:20,652
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I don't understand.

757
00:59:21,512 --> 00:59:21,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

758
00:59:22,132 --> 00:59:22,973
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll give it to the monkey.

759
00:59:31,098 --> 00:59:35,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if some of your thinking feels abstract, don't dismiss the thinking.

760
00:59:35,900 --> 00:59:37,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Just take it as, oh, there is a work to be done.

761
00:59:39,182 --> 00:59:44,845
[SPEAKER_01]: It's abstract only because my present experience, present bodily experience doesn't match it.

762
00:59:44,926 --> 00:59:45,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Can't comprehend it.

763
00:59:45,826 --> 00:59:47,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, comprehend it and you'll match it.

764
00:59:48,250 --> 00:59:50,972
[SPEAKER_00]: But the abstract thought of nothingness, is it fine?

765
00:59:51,152 --> 00:59:52,573
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a valid direction.

766
00:59:52,693 --> 00:59:53,593
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a valid direction.

767
00:59:53,614 --> 00:59:53,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

768
00:59:53,914 --> 00:59:55,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Based on what you read about nothingness.

769
00:59:56,255 --> 01:00:02,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, say, somebody, a completely different group, that doesn't follow any dhamma, starts talking about nothingness.

770
01:00:02,799 --> 01:00:15,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then, even that abstract thought of that nothingness that this other different, different, different school teacher is talking about, won't necessarily be the right direction either, because it's so much off.

771
01:00:16,313 --> 01:00:23,498
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you read the suttas and when the Buddha talks about nothingness, having the faith from the Buddha and so on, then, well, you can be pretty certain.

772
01:00:23,518 --> 01:00:34,267
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not have experienced nothingness, have certainly not been established upon it, but you can be pretty certain that at least the direction where your abstract thought took you is probably the right one.

773
01:00:35,127 --> 01:00:41,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's, you know, it's so vague in general, you really need to be seriously misled by instruction to go off that.

774
01:00:42,273 --> 01:00:45,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, of course, there is a lot of work to go into that direction.

775
01:00:45,915 --> 01:00:57,563
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, by reading the suttas, you can be rest assured that, well, at least the directions through your abstract thinking that you're getting, in terms of the instructions, are probably slightly right.

776
01:00:59,124 --> 01:01:03,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it's important to have the right instruction, no matter how general it is.

777
01:01:06,148 --> 01:01:08,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you were not to read the suttas, you'd read some other.

778
01:01:09,914 --> 01:01:13,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Stuff, whether presented as Buddhism or not, that can throw you off completely.

779
01:01:14,936 --> 01:01:15,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

780
01:01:16,956 --> 01:01:31,181
[SPEAKER_01]: But in this case, the suttas... when you read... and that's why, if you keep reading about the suttas, even without any other input or explanation, if you... obviously, with this kind of view that, okay, abstract thinking is a direction that I need to develop, where it ceases to be abstract.

781
01:01:34,162 --> 01:01:38,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if you keep reading the suttas, that direction will crystallize more and more.

782
01:01:39,419 --> 01:01:40,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you'll be thinking about it.

783
01:01:40,521 --> 01:01:41,343
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be pondering it.

784
01:01:41,864 --> 01:01:44,389
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be, as the Buddha said, you'll be investigating meaning.

785
01:01:45,191 --> 01:01:46,774
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be leaving anything unturned.

786
01:01:47,275 --> 01:01:48,257
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you'll be working, basically.

787
01:01:48,397 --> 01:01:49,139
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be walking.

788
01:01:49,975 --> 01:01:51,576
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, then you go east.

789
01:01:51,916 --> 01:01:56,417
[SPEAKER_01]: So you just start going east, but, you know, you can go far off in many directions that are east.

790
01:01:57,097 --> 01:01:58,078
[SPEAKER_01]: But you still keep going.

791
01:01:58,498 --> 01:02:01,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, the further you go, it will start narrowing down.

792
01:02:01,419 --> 01:02:03,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you get more signs, more instructions.

793
01:02:03,359 --> 01:02:08,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you recognize more of previous instructions that you heard, that you couldn't make much of at the time.

794
01:02:08,641 --> 01:02:12,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, your direction will get more and more focused and pointed out.

795
01:02:12,562 --> 01:02:13,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you're getting more precise.

796
01:02:15,437 --> 01:02:24,263
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that would be possible if you would have... if you would... if you... if you admit... sorry... if you dismissed the abstract thinking.

797
01:02:26,664 --> 01:02:28,726
[SPEAKER_01]: It is very arising because it's just thinking.

798
01:02:28,806 --> 01:02:29,566
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just abstract.

799
01:02:29,826 --> 01:02:30,507
[SPEAKER_01]: This is real.

800
01:02:30,607 --> 01:02:31,207
[SPEAKER_01]: That's abstract.

801
01:02:31,267 --> 01:02:31,427
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

802
01:02:32,368 --> 01:02:33,309
[SPEAKER_01]: That's equally real.

803
01:02:33,629 --> 01:02:36,571
[SPEAKER_01]: It's abstract only in regard to what you think it's more real.

804
01:02:37,011 --> 01:02:37,852
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're a putugena.

805
01:02:38,052 --> 01:02:41,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you should not be trusting what you think that it's more real.

806
01:02:41,964 --> 01:02:45,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if that is truly the real thing, you wouldn't be in a position.

807
01:02:45,107 --> 01:02:46,068
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be free from suffering.

808
01:02:46,088 --> 01:02:46,768
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be an ananta.

809
01:03:16,099 --> 01:03:17,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't dismiss abstract thinking.

810
01:03:17,981 --> 01:03:22,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to point it in the right direction through reading suttas and things that are least likely to be wrong.

811
01:03:24,166 --> 01:03:24,927
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, keep drilling.

812
01:03:26,729 --> 01:03:31,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's what I mean when I tell you, you have to be able to explain things to yourself.

813
01:03:32,334 --> 01:03:34,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't leave them vague, undefined.

814
01:03:34,696 --> 01:03:37,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it feels like this, or it feels like... No.

815
01:03:37,478 --> 01:03:38,199
[SPEAKER_01]: What does it mean?

816
01:03:39,980 --> 01:03:41,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing the meaning means understanding.

817
01:03:43,355 --> 01:03:46,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing that there is nothing to be understood in these particular things, means you understood them.

818
01:03:47,737 --> 01:03:50,258
[SPEAKER_01]: And they are not worth of understanding, of pursuing further.

819
01:03:53,319 --> 01:03:57,981
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, by reading the Suttas and Koan reading, you should be able to explain to yourself.

820
01:03:58,682 --> 01:04:02,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, it still feels abstract when I think about nothingness, but what it is?

821
01:04:03,123 --> 01:04:07,505
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my... What is the meaning of it for me to that... at that point?

822
01:04:07,725 --> 01:04:08,246
[SPEAKER_01]: What does it mean?

823
01:04:08,366 --> 01:04:09,266
[SPEAKER_01]: How did I understand it?

824
01:04:11,196 --> 01:04:20,481
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean you start going to some, like, psychotherapy, spiritual terminology, where you just start a while, it's like this, and it feels light, and it feels... No!

825
01:04:20,881 --> 01:04:22,002
[SPEAKER_01]: What does it mean?

826
01:04:22,022 --> 01:04:23,543
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not asking you to describe how you feel.

827
01:04:24,023 --> 01:04:24,584
[SPEAKER_01]: What does it mean?

828
01:04:24,624 --> 01:04:25,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is your understanding at?

829
01:04:27,585 --> 01:04:35,890
[SPEAKER_01]: And most importantly, is that understanding contributing to your freedom of suffering, and freedom from possibility to suffer, that is present?

830
01:04:37,918 --> 01:04:48,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the understanding pertains to that, if understanding feels directly related or directly correlated to the freedom from suffering, then carry on.

831
01:04:48,824 --> 01:04:50,204
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's irrelevant?

832
01:04:50,224 --> 01:04:51,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I feel like this, or I feel like that.

833
01:04:51,665 --> 01:04:52,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that makes no difference.

834
01:04:52,626 --> 01:04:53,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is there.

835
01:04:53,246 --> 01:04:54,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you suffer in regard to your feelings.

836
01:04:56,167 --> 01:04:56,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I feel...

837
01:04:57,088 --> 01:04:58,129
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel like Superman today.

838
01:04:58,169 --> 01:04:59,749
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel untouchable.

839
01:04:59,809 --> 01:05:00,690
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel imperturbable.

840
01:05:00,830 --> 01:05:01,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, great.

841
01:05:01,370 --> 01:05:02,971
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you going to do when your feeling ceases?

842
01:05:04,092 --> 01:05:06,273
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just establish your peace upon your feeling.

843
01:05:07,062 --> 01:05:15,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, the whole problem of not being free, not being peaceful, is depending upon feelings, being affected by them.

844
01:05:18,204 --> 01:05:19,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's not be equitable.

845
01:05:19,985 --> 01:05:21,005
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's not equitable.

846
01:05:21,025 --> 01:05:21,765
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

847
01:05:22,085 --> 01:05:27,866
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't just give in to any kind of flamboyant description of what you might feel like when you think about nothingness.

848
01:05:28,487 --> 01:05:30,867
[SPEAKER_01]: No, actually, develop it so you don't suffer on account of it.

849
01:05:30,947 --> 01:05:34,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, they wouldn't have laid it as a way out of suffering.

850
01:05:36,452 --> 01:05:41,338
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it doesn't pertain to freedom from suffering, means you're doing it wrong, or it's a wrong instruction.

851
01:05:41,358 --> 01:05:47,144
[SPEAKER_01]: But it since came from the Buddha, it's not the wrong instruction, thus you're doing it wrong.

852
01:05:47,164 --> 01:05:54,292
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always a good criteria of, okay, I've been pursuing this line of thinking in regard to base of nothingness.

853
01:05:54,813 --> 01:05:56,375
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been dwelling on the notions of this.

854
01:05:58,116 --> 01:06:00,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I know... can I suffer less on account of it?

855
01:06:00,718 --> 01:06:02,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you... like, even with your intelligence, you could see.

856
01:06:03,680 --> 01:06:08,344
[SPEAKER_01]: This seems that if I were to develop it, I would be untouchable by this unpleasant feeling when it arises.

857
01:06:09,305 --> 01:06:12,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that would also... because feeling is inseparable from perception.

858
01:06:13,088 --> 01:06:26,559
[SPEAKER_01]: And if I'm entirely surmounting the perception, and free from it, and established in that base that's higher than that perception, none of the feelings that arise on account of that perception I can perceive can affect me, because they pertain to perception, not to me, because I'm out of that perception.

859
01:06:31,211 --> 01:06:32,514
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, it is freeing you.

860
01:06:36,142 --> 01:06:37,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even less extra.

861
01:06:38,923 --> 01:06:43,346
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's abstract for somebody who's just... whose mind is far removed from it.

862
01:06:43,866 --> 01:06:48,229
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, as a thought, as a value direction, it must not be dismissed at any cost.

863
01:06:48,789 --> 01:06:52,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't necessarily follow it to such an extent, you can just go back to the obvious one.

864
01:06:53,592 --> 01:07:00,917
[SPEAKER_01]: On the perceptions of the forest, you abandon perceptions of the village, perceptions of the crowded life, perceptions of the people, perceptions of sensuality.

865
01:07:01,858 --> 01:07:05,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, pursue the perceptions which have a flavor.

866
01:07:06,414 --> 01:07:08,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Of freedom, the taste of freedom, isn't it?

867
01:07:08,815 --> 01:07:08,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

868
01:07:09,095 --> 01:07:23,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's... Yeah, these perceptions, I have not clearly developed them, but it seems to me, based on my limited discernment at this point, they're more likely to result in freedom than perceptions of hanging around the village.

869
01:07:24,083 --> 01:07:24,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

870
01:07:25,463 --> 01:07:26,784
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, I should follow them.

871
01:07:27,504 --> 01:07:31,206
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, by following them, you come closer to other perceptions that were there.

872
01:07:32,147 --> 01:07:33,888
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you actually... oh, yeah, no, I was right.

873
01:07:35,374 --> 01:07:37,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you define your taste of freedom.

874
01:07:38,955 --> 01:07:41,015
[SPEAKER_01]: You gain confidence in the taste of freedom.

875
01:07:43,716 --> 01:07:45,096
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you know, ah, it is free.

876
01:07:50,157 --> 01:08:03,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if abstract thinking of freedom would stand for that which freedom actually is, freedom from craving, freedom from suffering, all you have to do is keep repeating it, and you will be resolved upon it.

877
01:08:04,102 --> 01:08:05,203
[SPEAKER_00]: You're walking in that direction.

878
01:08:05,283 --> 01:08:16,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, you'll be resolved upon it, which means everything will be dragged into that direction, that third experience, because you anchored it there, and give it some time, and all the factors will fall off.

879
01:08:17,891 --> 01:08:20,832
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, yeah, this was right, even as an abstract thought.

880
01:08:21,153 --> 01:08:21,933
[SPEAKER_01]: It was very accurate.

881
01:08:23,674 --> 01:08:26,976
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, obviously, I was dismissing it, because I was so centered at the opposite end.

882
01:08:28,217 --> 01:08:29,658
[SPEAKER_01]: But fortunately, out of faith,

883
01:08:30,892 --> 01:08:31,572
[SPEAKER_01]: And persistence.

884
01:08:31,772 --> 01:08:34,594
[SPEAKER_01]: I kept doing it, and I had it rooted, I had it anchored there.

885
01:08:34,994 --> 01:08:36,734
[SPEAKER_01]: And look!

886
01:08:36,875 --> 01:08:47,859
[SPEAKER_01]: All this experience that was bogged down in sensuality, the mind that was fully controlled and just disturbed and abused by sensuality and everything else, has been dragged out.

887
01:08:47,979 --> 01:08:52,921
[SPEAKER_01]: And look, now it's there, where that imperturbable is, and all that swamp is abandoned.

888
01:08:52,941 --> 01:08:53,822
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes abstract.

889
01:08:54,162 --> 01:08:55,102
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's abstract now.

890
01:08:55,742 --> 01:08:56,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Shifts.

891
01:08:56,703 --> 01:08:56,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

892
01:08:58,347 --> 01:09:03,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you're thinking about sensuality that you have fully understood as pretty much inconceivable.

893
01:09:03,889 --> 01:09:05,810
[SPEAKER_01]: It's possible to... it's impossible to own.

894
01:09:06,470 --> 01:09:07,530
[SPEAKER_01]: That's abstract thinking.

895
01:09:09,231 --> 01:09:10,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are abstracted from it.

896
01:09:21,294 --> 01:09:27,638
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can see how much harm can be caused by the casual Buddhist advice that you often read and hear.

897
01:09:27,999 --> 01:09:28,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Or just stop thinking.

898
01:09:30,440 --> 01:09:33,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring all the contradictions that a sentence would imply.

899
01:09:34,743 --> 01:09:35,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, yeah.

900
01:09:35,424 --> 01:09:36,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts are a problem.

901
01:09:36,285 --> 01:09:37,665
[SPEAKER_01]: I stop thinking and I'm at peace.

902
01:09:44,050 --> 01:09:49,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you'll never try to think properly and you certainly will never be able to, well, to think.

903
01:09:50,472 --> 01:09:51,553
[SPEAKER_01]: To develop your mind.

904
01:09:52,734 --> 01:10:02,161
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, when I say think, I don't mean to sit and theorize or rationalize what makes sense to you in your putrugena-centered experience around your senses.

905
01:10:02,181 --> 01:10:05,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that the thinking is slave to the senses.

906
01:10:05,384 --> 01:10:06,345
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the other way around.

907
01:10:07,866 --> 01:10:08,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Or it should be, at least.

908
01:10:10,107 --> 01:10:13,050
[SPEAKER_01]: So, developing the thinking is based on the suttas, however abstract.

909
01:10:13,170 --> 01:10:15,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't dismiss it, but actually pursue their direction.

910
01:10:15,772 --> 01:10:17,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it will cease to be abstract.

911
01:10:19,063 --> 01:10:36,866
[SPEAKER_01]: One thing for sure, none of these states, space of nothingness, space of neither perception or perception, one intent upon nibbāna, will be arising by a chance, given to you by some meditative technique that kind of just made these things happen.

912
01:10:39,207 --> 01:10:39,447
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

913
01:10:40,667 --> 01:10:42,487
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop them through discerning what they are.

914
01:10:43,667 --> 01:10:47,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if your thought of what they are is accurate thought, that's it.

915
01:10:48,400 --> 01:10:49,201
[SPEAKER_01]: You are rooted in that.

916
01:10:49,221 --> 01:10:51,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you have to keep doing it long enough until all the fetters fade.

917
01:10:51,802 --> 01:10:52,543
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the right view.

918
01:10:54,024 --> 01:10:56,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Right view is the knowledge of nibbāna, knowledge of freedom from suffering.

919
01:10:59,067 --> 01:11:02,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you can pursue it, or you can choose to be distracted in regard to it, but you can't forget about it.

920
01:11:03,710 --> 01:11:09,834
[SPEAKER_01]: So, at most, in several lifetimes, you'll do away with all the fetters, but maybe even before.

921
01:11:10,995 --> 01:11:12,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what it talks about.

922
01:11:13,456 --> 01:11:14,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Sotāpanna is being reborn.

923
01:11:15,077 --> 01:11:17,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Not all of them need to sit through several lifetimes.

924
01:11:17,237 --> 01:11:21,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Some of them become arhants before they land into another river.

925
01:11:21,419 --> 01:11:23,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like a loose translation.

926
01:11:24,340 --> 01:11:25,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Or upon landing.

927
01:11:26,281 --> 01:11:28,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Or in the space in between, all those pali terms.

928
01:11:29,902 --> 01:11:36,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you could have taken to different faculties and then, even in that state in between, you carry on.

929
01:11:37,705 --> 01:11:39,246
[SPEAKER_01]: You carry on the development of that idea.

930
01:11:40,737 --> 01:11:43,718
[SPEAKER_01]: So, at most, several lifetimes, but not necessarily.

931
01:11:44,919 --> 01:11:47,900
[SPEAKER_00]: It's depending on how much effort... How much effort you put in.

932
01:11:48,020 --> 01:11:49,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

933
01:11:49,580 --> 01:11:53,882
[SPEAKER_01]: How accurate your thoughts of what nibbāna is actually are.

934
01:11:53,902 --> 01:11:56,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no... and that's one thing you realize.

935
01:11:57,063 --> 01:12:01,665
[SPEAKER_01]: What you think nibbāna is, can be right or wrong.

936
01:12:01,685 --> 01:12:07,567
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't have a reference point to it externally, whereby you see, okay, this is what nibbāna is, and now I can correct my thinking.

937
01:12:07,727 --> 01:12:07,907
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

938
01:12:08,327 --> 01:12:10,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can only see it from the inside.

939
01:12:11,124 --> 01:12:14,147
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will know that this is the right thinking when you're fully free from suffering.

940
01:12:14,247 --> 01:12:14,947
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you know, okay.

941
01:12:15,568 --> 01:12:17,870
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sometimes anāgāmī overestimated himself.

942
01:12:17,910 --> 01:12:19,851
[SPEAKER_01]: They thought they were fully nibbāna, but they were not quite.

943
01:12:20,332 --> 01:12:24,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the things get a bit subtler later on, in terms of conceit and so on.

944
01:12:24,956 --> 01:12:28,999
[SPEAKER_01]: So, some people might not necessarily see if the circumstances are not prompting them to see it.

945
01:12:30,620 --> 01:12:32,802
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, you can't have an external view of anything.

946
01:12:34,712 --> 01:12:41,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means then, your abstract thinking, or non-abstract thinking, is all you have to stand for that thing which you're aiming at for freedom.

947
01:12:43,155 --> 01:12:47,677
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I meant in the essay, Not Perceiving the Feeling and Determining Determinations.

948
01:12:48,157 --> 01:12:53,479
[SPEAKER_01]: That because of which you have thoughts of that, because of which you have thoughts.

949
01:12:54,219 --> 01:12:59,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can only think in regard to that which you're thinking, but that's already another thought that you're thinking.

950
01:13:00,761 --> 01:13:02,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can't step outside of your thought.

951
01:13:03,305 --> 01:13:04,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone that you should dismiss it.

952
01:13:06,168 --> 01:13:11,333
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you have to do then, is make sure you stop assuming that thought to be that which it isn't.

953
01:13:13,575 --> 01:13:16,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that thought will stand for that which that thought is.

954
01:13:20,014 --> 01:13:38,848
[SPEAKER_01]: In other way, have an accurate thought of what nibbāna is, have an accurate thought of what a way out of suffering is, have an accurate thought of what craving is, have an accurate thought of what matter is, have an accurate thought of what feeling is, have an accurate thought of what perception is, have an accurate thought of what intentions are, have an accurate thought of what consciousness is.

955
01:13:38,868 --> 01:13:41,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Because through an inaccurate thought, you introduce assumption.

956
01:13:43,351 --> 01:13:44,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's it.

957
01:13:44,112 --> 01:13:48,075
[SPEAKER_01]: The discrepancy of inaccuracy is the extent of your assumption.

958
01:13:49,799 --> 01:13:50,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Extend to your rūpa then.

